WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.020
- Welcome into another
edition of Market Takes.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:05.670
Look, I know you could have
been anywhere in the world

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:08.160
but you were here with
me and I appreciate that.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:11.220
I am Dion Rabouin, Reporter
at the Wall Street Journal

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:14.190
and you know there is a lot
going on this week in the world

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:16.170
of markets and economics,
but I'm gonna tell you

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:18.330
the three most important things

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.220
you need to be watching out for this week

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.720
and it is a big one.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:25.710
Look, this week we got the CPI
report, consumer price index,

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:28.890
the inflation report that's
really most dedicated

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.110
to what's going on with
consumers, you and me,

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:32.400
what we're doing, what we're spending

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:33.600
and how we're spending.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.600
That report is gonna show us
where inflation is landed.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:37.530
We get that tomorrow.

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:39.030
We will be right here for Market Takes.

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:40.050
I hope you'll be with us.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:42.390
Wednesday, the Federal Reserve meets

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:44.520
the FOMC policy rate decision.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:45.960
That's going on on Wednesday.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.540
We'll be right here again
on Market Takes at 4:00 PM,

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:49.860
Hope you'll join us then as well.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:52.620
And then Thursday we've got Retail sales.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:54.360
So we're gonna break
down what all those are,

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.880
what you should be watching
for each one of those reports.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:58.230
Let's start with CPI.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:00:59.250
Wanna put a chart up here

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:01.590
on your screen to just
kick things off with that.

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:05.910
Now we have seen inflation
cooling since the middle of 2022.

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:08.010
Now you see there on your
screen the chart rising.

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:09.330
You see those bars getting higher.

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:12.690
We reached 9% in June and ever since then

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.960
really inflation has been
coming down on the CPI.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.510
But you've seen there towards the end

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:20.970
the bar's not really
moving significantly lower.

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:24.420
You see a defined lower
numbers on each of those bars.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:27.330
And then the last couple months
have been right around 5%.

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:29.310
5% definitely lower than 9%

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:33.540
but very far from the Feds 2%
target, which it, you know,

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.160
we're seeing a more than double.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:38.730
So again, worrying that
inflation could be stuck

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:41.760
at this 5% level is
what's got some economists

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:42.600
a little worry about that.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:44.430
And that chart just
illustrating, like we said

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:46.950
that inflation had been
coming down consistently

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:48.000
but it stalled out there.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.490
And if it continues to stall
out, that could be trouble.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:52.050
That could mean the Feds gotta do more

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:53.700
raise interest rates even more,

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:56.460
do more to choke off and sort
of slow down the economy.

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:59.490
Economists however, are expecting that CPI

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:02.820
is gonna come down to 4% for this month,

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:04.860
or at least for last month in the report

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:06.180
that we get this month.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:08.610
4% which would be very
bullish for the market

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:10.830
and definitely bullish for stocks.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.440
Recent data, however, has
suggested that that inflation

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:14.790
cooling trend that we have seen

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:16.470
for most of this year could be slowing.

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:18.510
We've seen home sales starting to pick up,

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:21.060
particularly new home
sales reached the highest

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:23.400
since March, 2022 last month.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:26.070
We've also seen housing permits pick up

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:27.810
and home builder
confidence actually reached

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:30.360
a 10 month high last month.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:35.040
We have also seen inventory
of expected homes 44% lower

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.410
than it was pre pandemic, or 44% lower

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:39.420
than their pre pandemic levels.

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:41.700
We are also seeing
business activity rising

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:44.250
to a 13 month high according to the S&amp;P

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:45.900
purchasing managers index

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:49.590
and Retail sales rising surprisingly

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:51.810
last month when we expected a decline.

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:54.270
We will talk more about
that in just a moment.

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:56.370
So all these things have
suggested the consumer

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:58.230
continues to go out and spend.

00:02:58.230 --> 00:02:59.520
Folks are still spending money

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:01.380
even though prices are still rising

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:02.400
at a pretty fast clip,

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.250
remember 5% inflation is still much faster

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:07.950
than anything we have seen
in the past 20 years or so.

00:03:08.886 --> 00:03:10.500
So prices still continuing to rise.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:13.980
And also those net profit
margins, we saw for Q1

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:17.070
now that we've seen about
99% of S&amp;P 500 companies

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:18.450
report their earnings.

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:21.870
We have seen profit margins rise again

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:24.360
for most of those companies in Q1.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:27.180
If you wanna continue to
see growth for companies

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:28.560
they usually have to continue

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.080
to keep raising those prices in Q2.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.000
So we will see if that is continued.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.270
Definitely CPI will be a
report to keep your eye on.

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:38.370
Also, you'll wanna watch Core CPI.

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:39.540
That number has been higher

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:41.640
while we have seen CPI
continue to come down,

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:43.680
Core CPI has held higher.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.810
Usually you need Core CPI to come down

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:47.460
or the Fed starts to get worried.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.410
Definitely something
you'll wanna watch out for.

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:50.670
Which brings us to, of course

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:52.530
the next big thing to watch this week.

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:54.570
That is the FOMC policy meeting,

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:56.700
Federal Open Markets Committee.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.770
That's where they decide what
they're gonna do about rates.

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:01.320
Now this month it's not
about the actual decision,

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:03.570
it's not about is the Fed
gonna raise rates or not.

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:06.060
The market is convinced
the Fed is going to stop.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:08.040
The question is, is
that a pause or a skip?

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.890
Are they stopping because
they're gonna hold rates here

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:11.730
at this level

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:13.890
or are they just pausing really quickly

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:16.800
and then going to start raising
them again starting in July?

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:18.780
The market is not quite
certain about that.

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:20.730
We're locked in for a pause this week.

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:23.070
But when we get the summary
of economic projections

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:25.740
the dot plot, when we see the statement

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:27.930
and we hear from Fed chair Jerome Powell

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:29.490
that's what's gonna move the market.

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:31.020
So you're gonna watch out for those.

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:33.690
And again, we will be with
you after that to break down

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:35.790
everything that was said and
everything that happened.

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:38.130
We've seen some disagreement
coming into this meeting.

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:40.080
Some Fed officials say, look,

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:41.700
inflation's starting to pick back up.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:43.110
They point to those home sales number,

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:44.610
they point to the jobs growth,

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:46.860
they point to some of these
other data business sentiment

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:48.390
and all that, and they say, listen,

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:50.070
inflation's gonna pick back up.

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:51.510
We've gotta be proactive.

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:52.530
Even if we stop here

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:54.300
we've gotta start raising again in July.

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:56.310
While some other Fed officials say,

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:58.260
no, we may have already done too much.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:04:59.220
We've gotta pause,

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:00.510
we've gotta make clear to the market

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:03.420
that we're not gonna do too
much and really send the country

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:06.870
into a needless and unnecessary
avoidable recession.

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:09.180
So that's what you've got coming in.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:11.700
CPI will be a big
determinant of what that is

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:13.590
and how Fed officials respond.

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:15.930
And then we're gonna hear
from them on Wednesday.

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:18.840
So we are going to get a
chance to see what they say

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:20.160
and hear what they think.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:22.110
And then of course we'll
hear from the chairman

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:23.760
at 2:30 on Wednesday.

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:26.220
Markets no longer pricing in
multiple rate cuts this year.

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:29.670
In fact, markets are pricing
in a rate hike in July.

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:31.320
And then after that, we'll see

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:32.580
and we're gonna get a lot of clarity

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:34.980
on what that is going
to be from the market.

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:36.840
Wanna show you one more quick thing

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:38.490
and put a chart up here on your screen.

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:39.540
And this right here,

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:42.120
what you're seeing is
the former FANG stocks

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:42.990
no longer called FANG.

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:46.710
Obviously, Facebook is now
META, Google is now Alphabet.

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:50.580
But these big tech companies,
that's Alphabet, Apple,

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:53.520
Netflix, Nvidia, some
of those bigger names

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:56.820
that you've seen, this
chart is actually made up of

00:05:56.820 --> 00:06:00.570
Apple, SNOW, AMD, Tesla, Amazon,
Netflix, Microsoft, Google.

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:04.050
But you see that major
significant outperformance there

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:04.980
of those stocks,

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:08.790
those big tech names up
about 50% over the past year.

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:11.940
When you bring it into
just so far year-to-date

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:14.490
starting from January,
those stocks have risen more

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:18.083
than 70% while the equal weighted S&amp;P 500,

00:06:18.083 --> 00:06:21.600
the S&amp;P is weighted by
the biggest companies

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:22.500
have the biggest weights.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:24.150
But if you take that and you say, okay,

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:27.780
all 500 companies in the
S&amp;P have the same weighting.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:30.150
Well that index has not risen

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:32.790
but about 1% so far this year.

00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:35.160
And that is a significant
difference from what we've seen

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:37.650
from big tech, which is
what leads the index.

00:06:37.650 --> 00:06:40.620
And that's rising by
about 70% year to date.

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:43.980
So this chart is illustrating
the significant difference

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:45.780
in overall performance of the market.

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:48.210
Those big tech names having a huge year.

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:49.920
The rest of the market not really,

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:51.390
kind of being left behind.

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:54.180
Last thing we wanna talk
about is US Retail sales.

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:55.440
I wanna put one more chart up here.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:56.610
Actually we've got two more charts.

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:59.040
But first, let's put this
chart up here on your screen.

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:01.320
This is the month over month
change in Retail sales.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:02.850
Now if you look on that chart

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:05.520
you can see four of the last
six months have been negative

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:07.800
and you're seeing a very
choppy movement, right?

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:09.750
You're seeing some months
Retail sales are down

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:10.890
some months they're up.

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:12.150
Now I want to compare that to

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:15.600
let's show you the year over
year change in Retail sales.

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:17.400
Well there you get a
much more clear picture

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:18.240
of what's been happening.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:20.250
Retail sales have been slowing down

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:21.660
consistently year over year.

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:23.820
When you look at it, we're
seeing a slowdown now

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:26.850
to about 2.5% percent of the last reading.

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:29.370
And this does not factor in inflation.

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:31.200
So this is just how
much people are spending

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:32.280
compared to the year before.

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:33.990
So even though prices are going up

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:36.150
and overall sales are increasing, right,

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:38.370
you're seeing more sales
because prices are higher,

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:40.890
it's just about how much
money was brought in.

00:07:40.890 --> 00:07:43.710
Well, you're seeing a
decline in Retail sales.

00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:47.460
So even though we've still
got inflation up 5% last month

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:50.160
you saw Retail sales only
up about 2.5% percent.

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:52.200
And that shows you that
you're seeing a defined

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:54.300
and clear slowdown of the consumer.

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:55.380
How much does that continue

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:56.850
or how much does that trend change?

00:07:56.850 --> 00:07:59.010
We will find out on
Thursday and that chart

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:00.390
just illustrating what we had seen

00:08:00.390 --> 00:08:02.070
from Retail sales coming in.

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:04.920
So will consumers continue to spend?

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:06.840
How will the Fed react to the CPI data

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:09.060
and what will the CPI
inflation data tell you?

00:08:09.060 --> 00:08:10.950
We will find all that out this week.

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:14.280
It is a big week, the rubber
meeting the road in a big way.

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:16.920
So listen, that's gonna do it
for us here on Market Takes.

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:19.530
Thank you so much for looking
forward to the week with me.

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:21.960
I'm Dion Rabouin, Reporter
at the Wall Street Journal.

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:24.480
We will be with you
tomorrow morning at 10:00 AM

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:26.070
breaking down the CPI report.

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:28.820
I hope you'll be with us when
we do it again next time.

